---
export const prerender = true
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import { comics, anime, charactersWithPages } from '../lib/catalog.js'
import { UPDATES } from '../lib/site-updates.js'

const latest = UPDATES[0]

const jsonld = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'AboutPage',
  name: 'About manhwaindex',
  url: 'https://manhwaindex.com/about',
  description:
    'Who runs manhwaindex, how official links are chosen and checked, and how to report a wrong link.',
  publisher: {
    '@type': 'Organization',
    name: 'manhwaindex',
    url: 'https://manhwaindex.com',
    email: 'hello@manhwaindex.com',
    founder: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Shahid Ali' },
  },
}
---

<Base
  title="About manhwaindex — an index of official reading and streaming links"
  description="Who runs manhwaindex, how each official reading and streaming link is chosen and checked, and how to report a link that is wrong."
  canonical="/about"
  jsonld={jsonld}
>
  <div class="shell">
    <section class="section">
      <div class="section__head">
        <h1>About manhwaindex</h1>
        <p>One question, answered fast: where can I legally read or watch this?</p>
      </div>

      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Who runs it</h2>
        <p>
          manhwaindex is run by Shahid Ali as a hobby. It exists for one plain problem:
          finding the legal place to read a series means searching across many apps, and
          each one carries a different part of the market. This site does that search once
          and keeps the answer current.
        </p>
        <p>
          There is no company behind it and no publisher paying for a place on a page. The
          site is paid for by Amazon links and, later, ads. Neither one decides which
          platform a page shows first.
        </p>

        <h2>What is here</h2>
        <p>
          A catalog of {comics.length.toLocaleString()} comics and novels,
          {anime.length.toLocaleString()} anime and {charactersWithPages.length.toLocaleString()}
          characters. Each title page answers four questions: where it is official, whether
          it is free there, which language it is in, and what to read or watch next.
        </p>
        <p class="prose__note">
          We never host chapters or episodes. Every outbound link goes to a publisher, a
          licensor or a streaming service that holds the rights.
        </p>

        <h2>What is checked, and what is done by hand</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Only official platforms.</strong> A reading link appears only when it
            points at one of a fixed list of publishers and licensed apps. Everything else is
            left out, however popular.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Re-checked every six hours.</strong> Titles and links are compared with
            the source data four times a day, and a link that is gone from the source is
            gone from the page at the next check.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Reports are fixed by hand.</strong> A wrong or dead link that a reader
            reports is checked and fixed, and a missing title can be added on request.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Some titles are left out on purpose.</strong> Adult titles are never
            listed, and a title can be removed for good when its content crosses a line.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The full rules are on <a href="/how-we-check-links">How we check links</a>. What
          changed lately is on <a href="/updates">Updates</a>{latest && (
            <> (last change: <time datetime={latest.date}>{latest.dateText}</time>)</>
          )}.
        </p>

        <h2>Where the data comes from</h2>
        <p>
          Titles, synopses, cover art, scores and character profiles come from
          <a href="https://anilist.co" rel="noopener">AniList</a>, a community-run media
          database with a public API. Scores are cross-checked against
          <a href="https://myanimelist.net" rel="noopener">MyAnimeList</a>. Opening and
          ending songs come from AnimeThemes. Cover art belongs to its publishers and is
          shown to identify the title.
        </p>
        <p>
          What this site adds on top: which platforms are official, which are free, the
          language of each release, reading and watch order, reader ratings and reviews,
          and the "if you liked this" lists.
        </p>

        <h2>Corrections</h2>
        <p>
          Availability changes all the time. If a link is wrong or a title is missing a
          platform, write to <a class="mailto" href="mailto:hello@manhwaindex.com">hello@manhwaindex.com</a>
          or use the <a href="/contact">contact page</a>. Each report is read and checked.
        </p>
        <p>
          manhwaindex is independent. It is not affiliated with, endorsed by, or sponsored
          by AniList, MyAnimeList, or any publisher or streaming service named on this site.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
